Alembic — database migrations
=============================

Run every command from the **project root** (`C:\Projects\LinguaAI\`), passing
the ini file explicitly — NOT from `backend/`:

    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini upgrade head

`script_location` in `alembic.ini` is `%(here)s/alembic`, which resolves
relative to the ini file itself, so it works from any cwd. But
`DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./lingua_ai.db` (backend/.env) is a *relative* path
that SQLite resolves against the process's cwd at connect time — the app
always runs from the project root (see CLAUDE.md), so Alembic must too, or
it silently targets the wrong SQLite file (`backend/lingua_ai.db` instead of
the real one). This bit us once already (ACTION_PLAN.md F1-1, 2026-08-19) —
don't reintroduce `cd backend` into these instructions.

The target database is resolved from `DATABASE_URL` (env / backend/.env) by
`env.py`, falling back to the value in `alembic.ini`. So the same migrations
run against SQLite locally and PostgreSQL in deployment without editing any
file:

    # local (SQLite, uses the app's default DATABASE_URL)
    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini upgrade head

    # deployment (PostgreSQL)
    DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host/db py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini upgrade head

Common commands
---------------
    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini current            # revision the DB is at
    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini heads               # latest revision(s) in versions/
    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini history              # full revision graph
    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini check                # fail if models drift from migrations
    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini revision --autogenerate -m "add X"   # new migration
    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini upgrade head         # apply
    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini downgrade -1         # roll back one

App startup (2026-08-19 — ACTION_PLAN.md F1-4)
------------------------------------------------
`backend/main.py`'s lifespan now runs `alembic upgrade head` on every
startup (skipped when `TESTING=1`, since `backend/tests/conftest.py` manages
its own isolated `test_language_tutor.db` via `Base.metadata.create_all()`).
This replaced the old `Base.metadata.create_all()` + a hand-maintained list
of `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN` statements. A fresh install builds the whole
schema from the revision history; an existing install only gets the
revisions it's missing. No manual step is needed — this note documents what
already happens automatically, for whoever next reads this file wondering
why the schema is never behind.

Policy: zero manual `ALTER TABLE` from now on
----------------------------------------------
Every change to a model in `backend/models/*.py` **must** ship with an
Alembic revision in the same commit:

    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini revision --autogenerate -m "describe the change"

Review the generated file before committing — SQLite autogenerate is not
always precise (see `8dfdfadd8a31_fix_schema_drift_from_manual_alters.py`
and `9c1a1e9b7b4f_add_topics_hierarchy_and_mnemonic.py` for real examples of
hand-fixing an autogenerated migration: named constraints for SQLite batch
mode, and `sa.inspect()` existence guards so one migration is safe to run
against both a fresh install and an existing drifted database). Do not run
`sqlite3`/raw `ALTER TABLE` against `lingua_ai.db` directly, and do not add
new entries to any ad-hoc migration list — that pattern is exactly what
caused the Alembic history to drift out of sync with the real schema in the
first place (see `8dfdfadd8a31`'s docstring for the full incident).

Existing pre-Alembic databases (rare — only relevant if you have an old DB
that was never touched by the app after Alembic was introduced)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
If a database predates Alembic entirely and already has every table (built
purely by the old `Base.metadata.create_all()`), stamp it instead of
upgrading, to avoid Alembic trying to recreate tables that already exist:

    py -3.11 -m alembic -c backend/alembic.ini stamp --purge head

New databases (e.g. a fresh PostgreSQL in the cloud) should instead run
`upgrade head` to build the schema from the baseline migration.

Notes
-----
- `env.py` imports every model so autogenerate sees the full metadata. When you
  add a new model, add its import there too (same list as main.py's lifespan
  used to have, before F1-4 — check `backend/models/__init__.py` and the
  routers/services that import each model directly).
- SQLite migrations use batch mode (`render_as_batch`) because SQLite cannot
  ALTER most columns in place. No effect on PostgreSQL.
